---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import NnMasthead from '../../components/NnMasthead.astro';
import race from '../../content/race.json';
import { orTbc } from '@src/shared/privacy';

// The same event theme as the forms it belongs to — see packages/shared/styles/nn-theme.css.
//
// **The horror stops at the edge of the card.** The page furniture is the campaign's, so
// somebody arriving from a form knows they have not left it; the notice itself is ink on
// white, Inter throughout, with nothing decorative anywhere near the legal text.
import '@src/shared/styles/nn-theme.css';

// The race's privacy notice.
//
// ## This is the committee's document, published verbatim
//
// **The committee supplied this notice as a document, and on 30 August 2026 the club asked for
// it to be published word for word.** Every sentence below is theirs. Nothing here is written,
// corrected, extended or reordered, and that is the instruction rather than an oversight — so
// a sentence that looks wrong is changed by the committee supplying new wording, never by
// editing this file.
//
// **An earlier version of this page merged that document with the notice it replaced.** It
// carried a medical-note retention period, a schema-derived list of what the entry tables
// hold, the named processors, the registered office, and the affiliation sentence. All of it
// came out when the club asked for the document as it stands. Three of those differences are
// load-bearing elsewhere, so they are recorded here rather than left to be rediscovered:
//
//  1. **No retention period for a medical note is published.**
//     `entries.events.medical_retention` is still one month and the cron still deletes on it,
//     and `packages/db/tests/entries-retention.test.ts` still ties that interval to
//     `race.json`'s `privacy.medicalRetention` — which is why that key is still there and
//     still reads "One month after the race" though this page no longer prints it. **The tie
//     is now between the column and the JSON only**, and no longer between either and what a
//     runner reads.
//  2. **The medical box, the visually impaired declaration and the guide are not named.** All
//     three are collected — see `NN_ENTRY_FIELDS` — and the first two are special category
//     data under Article 9.
//  3. **The affiliation sentence is not here.** ADR-023 asks for it on both notices; it
//     remains on `/privacy/`.
//
// ## What is interpolated, and why that is not an edit
//
// Four values come from `race.json` rather than being typed into the markup: the controller,
// the company number, the contact address and the date. **They render exactly the words the
// document uses.** `/privacy/` reads the same four, so retyping them here would let the club's
// two notices disagree about who the controller is — which has to be impossible rather than
// merely unlikely. `orTbc` renders "to be confirmed by the club" for a `null`, and none of
// these four is null, so this page carries no marker at all now.
//
// **A wrong answer on this page is a legal claim, not a typo.** See
// docs/architecture/principles.md#stop-and-ask

const canonical = 'https://new.southvillerunningclub.co.uk/nn/privacy/';

const { privacy } = race;
---

<Base
  title="What the club does with your details — Nightingale Nightmare"
  description="What Southville Running Club collects when you enter Nightingale Nightmare or register your interest, why, who else sees it, and how long it is kept."
  canonical={canonical}
  theme="nn"
>
  {
    /* The campaign navigation, as on the other four pages. Somebody who opens the privacy
    notice to check what is held before typing their name should not have to retrace their
    steps to carry on. */
  }
  <NnMasthead slot="masthead" />

  <header class="nn-hero nn-hero-compact">
    <div class="nn-wrap">
      <h1>What the club does with your details</h1>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="nn-wrap">
    <div class="nn-card nn-prose">
      {
        /* **`{' '}` is a real space, and it is here because a line break is not.** A newline
        either side of an expression is collapsed away rather than kept as a space, so a
        paragraph reads "…Nightingale Nightmareand nothing else" without it. Prettier is what
        moves a bare `{expression}` onto a line of its own, so a space typed in the source does
        not survive the next format — this page has now met that trap three times.
        `nn-privacy.spec.ts` asserts these joins as whole strings. */
      }
      <p class="lede">
        This covers the entry form and the register-your-interest form for{' '}
        {race.name}. Everything else the club does with your details — including an
        account on this website — is in <a href="/privacy/">the club's privacy notice</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>1. Who we are</h2>

      <p>
        {orTbc(privacy.controller)} is committed to protecting and respecting your privacy.
        For any personal data you provide for the purposes of registering your interest or entering{
          ' '
        }
        {race.name}, {orTbc(privacy.controller)}, Companies House company no.{' '}
        {orTbc(privacy.companyNumber)} (SRC) is the Data Controller and is responsible for storing
        and otherwise processing that data in a fair, lawful, secure and transparent way.
      </p>

      <p>
        If you are unhappy with how we have handled your information, please contact us at{
          ' '
        }
        {
          /* **A link only when there is an address to link to.** `orTbc` exists because these
          values can be `null`, and `href="mailto:null"` is a link that looks real, is reached
          by keyboard, and opens a message to nobody. The marker is not an address and must not
          be dressed as one. The link also carries `base.css`'s
          `a[href^='mailto:'] { overflow-wrap: anywhere }`, which is what stops a 31-character
          address scrolling the page sideways at 320px — the defect `/privacy/` met on the
          same value. */
        }
        {
          privacy.contact ? (
            <a href={`mailto:${privacy.contact}`}>{privacy.contact}</a>
          ) : (
            orTbc(privacy.contact)
          )
        }. You also have the right to complain to the Information Commissioner's Office{
          ' '
        }
        <a href="https://ico.org.uk/">https://ico.org.uk/</a>
      </p>

      <h2>2. Information we collect</h2>

      <p>
        We collect the following types of information to manage race registration and
        event logistics:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Personal Identification:</strong> Name, date of birth, gender.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Contact Details:</strong> Email address, phone number, postal address.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Race-Related Information:</strong> Emergency contact details, running club
          affiliation, chosen race category, expected finish time.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Payment Information:</strong> Processed securely by our third-party payment
          provider; we do not store full card details.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Technical Data:</strong> IP address, browser type, device information, and
          cookies for website functionality and analytics.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>3. How We Use Your Information</h2>

      <p>We use your information to:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Process your race registration and payment</li>
        <li>Communicate event updates and essential safety information</li>
        <li>Manage timing, results, and race-day logistics</li>
        <li>Provide customer support</li>
        <li>Improve website performance and user experience</li>
        <li>Comply with legal or regulatory obligations</li>
      </ul>

      <p>We do not sell your data to third parties.</p>

      <h2>4. Legal Basis For Processing</h2>

      <p>We process your data under the following legal bases:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Contractual necessity: To register you for the race and deliver event services
        </li>
        <li>
          Legitimate interests: Event safety, operational efficiency, and fraud prevention
        </li>
        <li>Consent: For optional marketing communications</li>
        <li>Legal obligations: Health and safety requirements, incident reporting</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>5. Data Sharing</h2>

      <p>We may share your information with:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Timing and results providers</li>
        <li>Payment processors</li>
        <li>Medical or emergency services (only when necessary for safety)</li>
        <li>
          Our race is run under an ARC permit, so we may have to share limited details
          with the permitting body if there is an incident.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>6. Data Retention</h2>

      <p>
        We retain information only for as long as reasonably necessary for the purposes
        described in this policy, including legal, accounting, insurance,
        dispute-resolution, safety, and historical-results requirements.
      </p>

      <p>
        When the retention policy ends, we will securely delete the information, or remove
        any personally identifiable information.
      </p>

      <h2>7. Your rights</h2>

      <p>You can have the right to</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Access your personal data</li>
        <li>Request correction or deletion</li>
        <li>Object to processing</li>
        <li>Withdraw consent</li>
        <li>Request data portability</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        <strong
          >If you request data deletion, your information will be removed and your race
          entry will be cancelled.</strong
        > Requesting deletion of your data will not impact any future relationship with the
        SRC. Please contact {
          privacy.contact ? (
            <a href={`mailto:${privacy.contact}`}>{privacy.contact}</a>
          ) : (
            orTbc(privacy.contact)
          )
        }.
      </p>

      <h2>8. Photographs</h2>

      <p>
        Photographs will be taken throughout the event for the purposes of documenting
        race activities, celebrating participant achievements, and promoting future
        events. This may include images of runners, volunteers, spectators, and the
        general event environment. By attending you acknowledge that you may appear in
        these images. We may use selected photos on our website, social media channels,
        marketing materials, and partner platforms, but we will never use imagery in a
        misleading or harmful way. If you prefer not to be photographed, please inform us
        before the event and avoid posing for official photographers; however, incidental
        inclusion in wide-angle or crowd shots may still occur.
      </p>

      <h2>9. Changes to this policy</h2>

      <p>
        We may update this Privacy Policy periodically. Any changes will be posted on this
        page with an updated revision date.
      </p>

      <p>Last updated: {orTbc(privacy.lastUpdated)}</p>
    </div>

    {
      /* **The sign-off paragraph was removed on 30 August 2026, at the club's request.** It
      read "Anything above reading 'to be confirmed by the club' is with the committee, and this
      page is updated when it is settled" — a sentence describing a page with unanswered values
      on it. There are none left here, so it described nothing. The two links stay: a notice
      somebody cannot get back out of is a dead end. */
    }
    <footer>
      <p>
        <a href="/nn/">Back to {race.name}</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/privacy/">The club's privacy notice</a>
      </p>
    </footer>
  </div>

  {
    /* The same decorative artwork as the forms' page, and empty for the same reason — it is
    a CSS background. See the artwork section of nn-theme.css. */
  }
  <div class="nn-hands" aria-hidden="true"></div>
</Base>
